    "result": "The electronics dataset is **g2** — its `main_cat` values are dominated by `All Electronics`, `Camera & Photo`, `Cell Phones & Accessories`, `Car Electronics`, etc., whereas g1 is dominated by `Office Products` and g3 by `Musical Instruments`. In `items_g2`, `prc` is the listed price in **cents** (e.g. `7499` → `$74.99`), and `prc_usd = prc/100`. Since 224 rows have `prc` but a null `prc_usd` (including the max-priced item), I averaged the complete `prc` column and converted to dollars.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"answer\": \"$96.23\",\n  \"sql\": \"SELECT AVG(prc) / 100.0 AS avg_listed_price_dollars FROM items_g2 WHERE prc IS NOT NULL\"\n}\n```\n\nThe average listed price of the 6,570 electronics products with a listed price is **$96.23**.\n\n(Note: averaging the pre-converted `prc_usd` column over its 6,346 non-null rows gives $52.96, but that understates the true mean because the 224 unsampled conversions are not missing at random — they include the single most expensive item at $26,888. Using the complete `prc` column is the correct computation.)",
